Per-worker test time sums to more than the elapsed wall clock because the
runner distributes individual test IDs, so a class fixture runs once per test
method rather than once per class. That is the cost of every test being
independently addressable and sharing no state.

## What this run covers that the last one did not

Whether the closure notices being pointed at something else. Excluding whole
manifest objects because they contained a path meant `sources`, `tools` and the
whole of `fixture` were outside the identity, so changing which board the
release builds, or emptying `fixture.reject_globs`, left the source-closure
digest untouched. The exclusion list is now exactly the leaves derive_manifest
assigns - a test reads them back out of derive_manifest's own source and
requires the two to agree - and a board may name a subset of it and nothing
more. The files named in `sources` are added to the closure by name, so a board
selected from a directory the globs exclude is still covered, and a selected
source that does not exist is refused rather than quietly missing.

Whether the frozen fixture reproduces anywhere. Its nested `.gitattributes`
said `* text=auto`, which beat the root's `-text` rule for the paths under it,
so the twenty frozen production artifacts were stored LF-normalised while
HASHES.json recorded the CRLF bytes they were frozen from. The fixture
therefore verified only on a checkout that happened not to rewrite them. The
nested file now says `generated/release/** -text` and the twenty blobs were
restored to the exact bytes already recorded; only the attributes file's own
inventory entry changed.

Whether the mutation harness runs anywhere. `_stub_cli` wrote a Windows batch
file unconditionally, and two tests invoked the absolute Windows KiCad path out
of the board manifest, so on any other machine they failed for the wrong
reason. The stub is rendered per platform now, and those two tests use
`tests/fake_kicad_cli.py` - a fake that reads the same project file KiCad reads
and reports the same ignored checks, so "edit the project, the gate fails
naming the rule" still holds without KiCad being installed.

Whether any of this works on a machine that is not this one. An independent
run of the previous commit from a fresh Linux checkout found four things that
were true only here: evidence paths matched with `os.path.basename`, which
turns a Windows path into one long filename on Linux and loses the archive the
report validated; report source hashes taken over raw bytes, so a checkout with
the other line ending looked stale before anyone had edited anything; a
recursive schematic glob that swept the validator's own fixtures and every past
attempt into the closure, so its value depended on what was left on disk; and a
mutation-test class that skipped entirely when no previous release happened to
be lying around.

`tests/test_source_closure.py` fixes the closure in place: it builds the same
project twice, once with LF and once with CRLF, and requires one identity while
a real edit still changes it; it requires the origin and clean-room
configuration identities to survive the path rewrites a clean room must make;
and it requires nothing under `verification/`, `generated/`, `build/` or
`candidates/` to be in the live closure at all. The release itself now compares
the two closures and refuses to publish if they disagree.

`tests/test_release_coherence.py` matches evidence recorded in Windows, POSIX
and mixed-separator forms - on this host, without pretending it is another one -
and requires a wrong digest to still fail in each. Its swap tests build a second
internally coherent package rather than looking for one in `verification/out`,
so they run in a checkout with no history and never skip. And a new test injects
an incoherence after the receipt is written, which is after every gate has
passed, and requires the release to refuse to publish it.


Whether the release package is one release. It was not: a validation report had
been refreshed on its own and left beside an archive from an earlier run, with
a note explaining the discrepancy. The swap tests described above take the
archive, the release manifest, a check report, the clean-room record and the
validation report from a second package that is itself completely coherent, and
put each in turn beside the installed one. Every file is well formed and every
swap must be caught. One test writes the prose note next to the mismatch and
requires it to fail anyway, because that is what was tried before. Others
remove each check report, reissue a receipt that accurately inventories what is
left, and require the package to be rejected as incomplete anyway.

Whether the code that derives the offsets is pinned. An offset is computed, not
read, so `pcbqa.orientation`, `pcbqa.cleanroom` and `pcbqa.gates.g_orientation`
are hashed from the modules that were imported and bound into the source
closure. Tests require the recorded digest to equal the loaded module's, an edit
to the implementation to change the closure digest, and a module dropping out of
the declared set to fail the gate. `tools/jlc_orientation.py` is checked
differently, and now correctly: it is loaded from the project under validation
by path rather than by name, and provenance compares the content of the file
that ran against the closure entry. Comparing `sys.modules` instead made the
answer depend on which project a worker had loaded first, and failed a release
that was fine.

Whether "reviewed" means reviewed. The status is now compared as an exact JSON
string with no trimming and no coercion, and eighteen near misses -
`" reviewed "`, `"reviewed\n"`, `"Reviewed"`, `null`, `true`, `1`, a list, a
dict - are each required to block generation and name the part.

And four things about placement orientation that an earlier run took on trust.

*The evidence is committed, not described.* `fabrication/jlc_orientation/raw/`
now holds each EasyEDA response body byte for byte, and the normalised extract
beside it is re-derived from that body every time it is read. Tests damage each
of the three things an edit could reach - the body, the extract, the registry's
offset - and require each to fail. One test replaces the extract's pads with
their negations and asserts the scored offset does not move, because scoring
reads the body.

*Only finished reviews ship.* `review_status` must be exactly `reviewed`.
Mutation tests set it to `unreviewed`, `pending`, an empty string, a
near-miss like `Reviewed`, and remove the field entirely; each must block
generation and fail the gate, naming the part.

*The range is half-open.* The validator used to allow exactly 360 through a
tolerance on the upper bound, which contradicted the file it was checking.
Boundary tests require 0 and 359.9999 to pass, 360 and -90 to fail, and
normalisation never to return the open end - including the case where rounding
to four decimals would carry 359.99999 up to it.

*The refusal is end to end.* The earlier regression test drove generation and
the gate separately, which cannot show that a release is impossible.
`ACleanReleaseRefusesAnUnreviewedPart` copies the project, removes `C7668` -
U2's part number - from the registry, and calls the same `cmd_release` entry
point a real release uses. It requires a non-zero result naming the part and
the missing mapping, no published directory, no `latest.json`, and the release
committed in this repository to be byte-identical afterwards. It takes about 90
seconds, which is what an end-to-end test of a release costs.

One test is sensitive to machine load rather than to the code:
`tests.test_runner.RunnerContract.test_concurrent_tests_never_share_an_output_directory`
asserts that four staggered tests reach more than one worker, which does not
hold when the machine is already saturated. It passes on an idle machine.
